(Bug #75, the map half. LANGUAGE.md's Maps section makes the store-back an
 explicit promise - "The map may reallocate on growth, so the returned
 pointer is stored back into the variable automatically" - and a parameter
 is a variable. It was not: a map grown through a `map` parameter stopped
 at eight entries, the size `_map_insert` gives a fresh map.)

To 'store into' with a map called holder and a text called label.
    Set holder's "{label}" to 1.

To 'set to two' with a map called holder and a text called label.
    Set holder's "{label}" to 2.

a map called m is {"seed": 0}.
a number called n is 0.
While n is less than 20,
    'store into' of m and "k{n}",
    Set n to n add 1.
Print "one entry, 20 calls: {m's length}".

(The entries are real, not just a count: the last one written reads back,
 and so does the one the literal seeded.)
a number called seeded is m's "seed".
a number called nineteenth is m's "k19".
Print "seed: {seeded}".
Print "k19: {nineteenth}".

(Replacing an existing key through the parameter still replaces rather than
 grows - the store-back must not turn an insert into a duplicate.)
'set to two' of m and "k3".
a number called third is m's "k3".
Print "after replace: {m's length}".
Print "k3: {third}".

(A map that never outgrows its first block was always right, and stays right.)
a map called small is {"a": 1}.
'store into' of small and "b".
'store into' of small and "c".
Print "small: {small's length}".
